<h2>Builders are fine until they are not</h2>
<p>Wix and Squarespace improved a lot. For a hobby blog or a temporary event page, they are reasonable. For a Michigan service business whose revenue depends on phone calls and map visibility, templates often become a monthly tax with limits. I rebuild owners off builders into Webstar custom sites at $350–$500 and up — not because builders are evil, but because ownership and speed matter when you compete in Royal Oak or Livonia search results.</p>

<h2>What DIY platforms do well</h2>
<p>Drag-and-drop control without hiring anyone. Built-in hosting on one bill. Decent themes for portfolios and simple stores. Quick experiments before you commit to a brand. If you are testing an idea for ninety days, a builder trial may be enough.</p>

<h2>Where Michigan small businesses feel pain</h2>
<p>Renewal pricing jumps after promo years. Mobile performance suffers when widgets stack — common for restaurants with menus, maps, and pop-ups. Export options are limited; moving means rebuild anyway. Google Analytics setup is easy to get wrong under the wrong property owner email.</p>
<p>Local SEO details — service areas, schema, clean URLs — are harder than ads suggest. You end up paying for SEO apps on top of hosting.</p>

<h2>Custom Simple Website: what changes</h2>
<p>Webstar Simple Website delivers three pages, forms, call buttons, and Google setup for $350–$500 one-time plus hosting you choose. You get semantic HTML and lightweight CSS — pages that load fast on cellular along I-75. You own files at handoff and can host on Webstar Care or any cPanel provider.</p>
<p>Design is still custom to your brand colors and photos, not a generic template with your logo swapped in.</p>

